1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,841
The Raptors: built for speed, and hunting as if they share one brain.

2
00:00:04,061 --> 00:00:05,341
Here's how they play.

3
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:10,299
Shuffle the eight Spawn cards into a face-down deck, and put the Raptors card beside

4
00:00:10,299 --> 00:00:12,119
it with the six Raptor meeples on it.

5
00:00:12,339 --> 00:00:16,332
The red Pack meeple goes on the Killer start space from the Setup card, instead of

6
00:00:16,332 --> 00:00:17,501
the usual Killer meeple.

7
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:22,410
The Pack meeple is a large, coordinated group of Raptors, and it counts as the

8
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:23,560
Killer for everything.

9
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,940
Its health is the number of Raptors in it.

10
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:30,054
It starts at five, a Final Health token plus four Health markers, but there's no

11
00:00:30,054 --> 00:00:30,671
upper limit.

12
00:00:30,891 --> 00:00:35,110
Its Attack Value on the Killer board is how much damage the Pack does in one attack,

13
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:36,416
however big the Pack gets.

14
00:00:36,636 --> 00:00:40,391
Eliminate the Pack and you win, even if loose Raptors are still on the board.

15
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,257
The green meeples are individual Raptors.

16
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:47,320
They're Minions for all purposes, with one health each, and they use the Movement

17
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:48,624
Value on the Killer board.

18
00:00:48,844 --> 00:00:52,603
They don't attack often, but when they do, each one attacks separately for one

19
00:00:52,603 --> 00:00:52,940
damage.

20
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:57,258
Read cards carefully: an effect on Raptors means only the loose Raptors, not the

21
00:00:57,258 --> 00:01:00,435
Pack, and an effect on the Pack doesn't include loose Raptors.

22
00:01:01,267 --> 00:01:05,380
Loose Raptors want to rejoin the Pack. When you see the join icon, they target the

23
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:07,588
Pack for movement, taking the shortest path.

24
00:01:07,588 --> 00:01:11,601
If paths tie, they take the one that gets them closest to the Pack before they'd

25
00:01:11,601 --> 00:01:12,253
have to stop.

26
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:16,641
They feel exposed alone, so they stop the moment they enter a space with you or a

27
00:01:16,641 --> 00:01:19,265
Victim, and won't move at all if they start in one.

28
00:01:19,265 --> 00:01:20,345
That's the stop icon.

29
00:01:20,565 --> 00:01:24,779
Location Terror cards don't show that icon, but it still applies whenever loose

30
00:01:24,779 --> 00:01:25,472
Raptors move.

31
00:01:25,692 --> 00:01:28,573
When Raptors reach the Pack's space they join immediately:

32
00:01:28,573 --> 00:01:32,697
take the meeples back to the Raptors card and add one Health marker to the Pack for

33
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:32,945
each.

34
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:38,021
When you resolve the egg icon, draw a Spawn card and place that many loose Raptors.

35
00:01:38,021 --> 00:01:41,148
Roll a die and check the table on the Raptors card for where.

36
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:45,295
On a one, they spawn in your space. Two and three put them in the closest space of

37
00:01:45,295 --> 00:01:49,271
the type shown to the Pack; four and five in the farthest such space from the Pack;

38
00:01:49,271 --> 00:01:51,139
and six in the space farthest from you.

39
00:01:51,359 --> 00:01:53,729
If more than one space qualifies, you choose.

40
00:01:53,729 --> 00:01:56,625
They can't spawn in a space that's no longer reachable.

41
00:01:56,625 --> 00:01:58,100
Then discard the Spawn card.

42
00:01:58,933 --> 00:02:03,091
If you run out of Raptor meeples, the Raptor closest to the Pack joins it straight

43
00:02:03,091 --> 00:02:06,336
away, and you reuse that meeple. Repeat until the spawn is done.

44
00:02:06,556 --> 00:02:09,863
If the Spawn deck runs out, shuffle the discards into a new deck.

45
00:02:10,083 --> 00:02:14,123
The final space of the Bloodlust track also spawns a Raptor, as if you'd drawn a

46
00:02:14,123 --> 00:02:15,032
Spawn card of one.

47
00:02:15,252 --> 00:02:18,679
And a Raptor spawned during the Killer phase doesn't act until the next Killer

48
00:02:18,679 --> 00:02:18,943
phase.

49
00:02:19,767 --> 00:02:23,287
Blood Thirsty: each time the Pack kills a Victim, its health goes up.

50
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:27,536
Pack Conditioning lets loose Raptors in adjacent spaces join the Pack without

51
00:02:27,536 --> 00:02:30,153
entering its space. It's the most punishing power:

52
00:02:30,153 --> 00:02:32,979
players win under sixty per cent of games with it out.

53
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:35,972
They're Everywhere makes egg symbols always spawn Raptors.

54
00:02:36,192 --> 00:02:40,672
And the Epic Dark Power, Scaly Hide: the Pack and the Raptors ignore all damage from

55
00:02:40,672 --> 00:02:43,019
Reaction cards, so Retaliate won't help you.

56
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:46,969
In most Finales the Pack moves at full speed and attacks hard.

57
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:51,051
Claws Like Knives: the first damage from each of its attacks can't be stopped.

58
00:02:51,271 --> 00:02:55,217
The Epic Finale, Deadly Quick: when revealed, the Pack is placed in your space

59
00:02:55,217 --> 00:02:58,353
immediately, and you lose four of the resource the card shows.

60
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:01,919
A few tips. Thin the Pack out early, before it snowballs.

61
00:03:02,139 --> 00:03:06,192
Use weak attacks on loose Raptors, which only have one health, and save your big

62
00:03:06,192 --> 00:03:09,029
weapons for the Pack. Kill strays before they rejoin it.

63
00:03:09,249 --> 00:03:11,826
They're tough: players win about two games in three.

64
00:03:11,826 --> 00:03:12,321
Good luck.

